[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Criteria API (Type-safe Queries) in Hibernate"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Criteria API in JPA and Hibernate provides a programmatic way to build SQL-like queries. Unlike JPQL or HQL, which rely on string manipulation and are evaluated at runtime, the Criteria API is type-safe and catches errors at compile time."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Why Use Type-safe Criteria Queries?"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Compile-time safety prevents typos in entity attribute names from slipping into production."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Dynamic query construction becomes cleaner and less prone to string-concatenation errors."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Refactoring tools in modern IDEs automatically update references to entity attributes used in Criteria queries."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting Up the Metamodel"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"To achieve strict type-safety, JPA utilizes a canonical metamodel. For an entity named 'Employee', an annotation processor generates a corresponding 'Employee_' metamodel class containing static volatile attributes."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"@Entity
public class Employee {
    @Id
    private Long id;
    private String name;
    private String department;
    private Double salary;
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Building a Type-safe Query"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"The following example demonstrates how to retrieve employees whose department is 'Engineering' and salary is greater than a specified threshold, utilizing the JPA CriteriaBuilder and Metamodel."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"CriteriaBuilder cb = entityManager.getCriteriaBuilder();
CriteriaQuery<Employee> query = cb.createQuery(Employee.class);
Root<Employee> root = query.from(Employee.class);

Predicate deptCondition = cb.equal(root.get(Employee_.department), "Engineering");
Predicate salaryCondition = cb.greaterThan(root.get(Employee_.salary), 75000.0);

query.select(root).where(cb.and(deptCondition, salaryCondition));

List<Employee> results = entityManager.createQuery(query).getResultList();"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always configure the Hibernate annotation processor in your build tool (Maven or Gradle) to generate metamodel classes automatically."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reuse CriteriaBuilder and Metamodel references where applicable, though EntityManager instances should remain scoped appropriately."}]
